The Nasdaq traded at 60× forward P/E before the dot-com bust. Today, it’s at 17×, its cheapest in 4 years. Sure, whether earnings hold up is debatable. But this is a fear-driven drawdown, not a fundamentals-driven one. Enough with the lazy dot-com comparisons and fearmongering. https://t.co/u0EGSju0JY

From an actionable standpoint, if the Nasdaq falls another 3–5%, my portfolio will consist entirely of Nasdaq exposure. My investment horizon will be one year, and I have no doubt I’ll outperform any other portfolio over that period.

If you’re an investor overwhelmed by all the noise and wondering whether to sell your stocks, especially your tech stocks, it’s too late. I’m not saying the Nasdaq can’t fall another 3–5%. It absolutely can. But I’m almost certain the next 10% move will be up, not down. This is pure seasonality and fear mongering.

@mishafrom Hyperscalers make up a significant portion of the Nasdaq, don’t they? I can assure you, Mr. Nasdaq would be happy to see some rationality return to their CapEx spending.

@warrqr I have no problem with anyone disagreeing with me or criticizing my past mistakes and poor decisions, as long as it’s done respectfully. In fact, I welcome it.

@skewpilot I think going long $TQQQ here is your best bet, especially given its daily rebalancing mechanism. If we’re right that Nasdaq volatility is about to decline and the index moves higher, $TQQQ could benefit from both the rebound and reduced volatility drag.

@Skeptical1704 $TQQQ is the right choice

@Fountainhead001 I understand what you’re saying. That’s basically what everyone is saying. But the forward P/E is at 17 right now, while during the dot-com bubble it was at 60. Do you understand the difference? One fugazi has some margin of safety. The other fugazi did not.

@EzraAll87018005 @mishafrom Don’t get caught up in the micro details of these events. They’re not useful to your investment process and only add noise. Just ask yourself one simple question: Is market sentiment toward tech currently overly bullish, neutral, or overly bearish? What do you think?

@investnyourself That's a viable way to view things.

